<section class="section" id="products">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <span class="eyebrow">Our products</span>
    <h2>Instruments for quality that <em>holds up</em>.</h2>

    <div class="products-grid">
      <article class="product-card">
        <div class="product-head">
          <h3>Qualgraph</h3>
        </div>
        <p>Teams have requirements, test results, bug reports and application data spread across different tools. Connecting that information takes time, yet the questions are simple: What have we verified? Where are the gaps? What should we check next?</p>
        <p>Qualgraph helps answer those questions by checking recorded application behavior against agreed rules and bringing supported evidence together into a clear review. It shows what was checked, what was found and where more evidence is needed.</p>
        <p>It also retains reviewed findings and quality history, helping teams compare results over time and carry useful knowledge into future reviews. AI can assist with analysis and explanation. People remain responsible for decisions.</p>
        <a class="card-link" href="/qualgraph">Learn more about Qualgraph →</a>
      </article>

      <article class="product-card">
        <div class="product-head">
          <h3>Warrant</h3>
        </div>
        <p>Warrant focuses on AI systems that make or recommend decisions. It helps businesses answer two questions:</p>
        <ul class="card-list">
          <li><b>How reliable are the checks on the AI’s decisions?</b> Warrant measures how closely an AI checker agrees with the organization’s experts, and whether that agreement changes when the model changes.</li>
          <li><b>What is the AI authorized to do?</b> Warrant records the scope of its permission, who approved it, when it expires and the supporting evidence, so an auditor can review the record independently.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="card-note"><span>Ownership</span> The organization runs Warrant and owns the results.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="product-card">
        <div class="product-head">
          <h3>Reality Lab</h3>
        </div>
        <p>Reality Lab is a controlled testing environment being developed to help teams see how software behaves in realistic situations, including when things go wrong. It runs defined scenarios and records what happened, giving teams evidence they can inspect to understand failures and check fixes. It complements Qualgraph and Warrant by providing a place to test the products and the checks themselves.</p>
        <p class="card-note"><span>Today</span> Its use is limited to controlled development testing; broader enterprise simulation and independent certification remain future capabilities.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="who">
      <span class="eyebrow">Who it's for</span>
      <h3 class="who-title">Built for the people who own the <em>quality bar</em>.</h3>
      <div class="aud-grid">
        <div class="aud">
          <div class="role">QA &amp; Quality Leaders</div>
          <h3>Prove the bar is real.</h3>
          <p>Move past "we tested it" to evidence you can defend — where decisions held, where they slipped, and what the signals were telling you all along.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="aud">
          <div class="role">Engineering Leaders</div>
          <h3>Ship fast without shipping blind.</h3>
          <p>Keep velocity while making every sign-off traceable. When something gets through, you can reconstruct exactly how — and close the gap.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
